WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.150 --> 00:00:01.529
Hi there, and thanks so much for

00:00:01.530 --> 00:00:02.699
clicking on this video.

00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:04.109
My name is Bradley Knapp and I'm

00:00:04.110 --> 00:00:05.399
with IBM Cloud.

00:00:05.400 --> 00:00:07.079
And the topic we're going to discuss

00:00:07.080 --> 00:00:09.809
today is what is an RCA,

00:00:09.810 --> 00:00:12.171
or a "Root Cause Analysis".

00:00:13.560 --> 00:00:15.569
All right. So to start off

00:00:15.570 --> 00:00:17.819
with, an RCA is a standard

00:00:17.820 --> 00:00:19.709
process that you should go through

00:00:19.710 --> 00:00:21.809
any time within the technology

00:00:21.810 --> 00:00:23.759
industry that you have what I like

00:00:23.760 --> 00:00:25.859
to call a customer impacting event,

00:00:25.860 --> 00:00:27.989
or a serious event where

00:00:27.990 --> 00:00:29.789
something has gone wrong and it has

00:00:29.790 --> 00:00:31.619
resulted in serious

00:00:31.620 --> 00:00:33.989
problems for your customers.

00:00:33.990 --> 00:00:35.879
It could be a down time outage.

00:00:35.880 --> 00:00:37.289
It could be a loss of network

00:00:37.290 --> 00:00:38.399
connectivity.

00:00:38.400 --> 00:00:40.289
It could be a loss

00:00:40.290 --> 00:00:41.969
of electricity.

00:00:41.970 --> 00:00:44.219
But no matter what the problem

00:00:44.220 --> 00:00:46.439
is, this RCA process,

00:00:46.440 --> 00:00:48.449
which is seven steps

00:00:48.450 --> 00:00:50.309
that this RCA process is

00:00:50.310 --> 00:00:51.899
designed to help you not only

00:00:51.900 --> 00:00:54.209
identify what the problem is,

00:00:54.210 --> 00:00:56.069
but how to fix it so that it doesn't

00:00:56.070 --> 00:00:56.939
ever happen again.

00:00:56.940 --> 00:00:59.129
So with that in mind,

00:00:59.130 --> 00:01:00.029
let's jump right in.

00:01:00.030 --> 00:01:01.859
Right. So the first step

00:01:01.860 --> 00:01:03.779
in an RCA, and I know this seems

00:01:03.780 --> 00:01:05.639
basic but it is the first and most

00:01:05.640 --> 00:01:08.909
important step, is you must

00:01:08.910 --> 00:01:11.069
identify

00:01:11.070 --> 00:01:12.419
what went wrong, right?

00:01:12.420 --> 00:01:14.429
You have to identify your problem.

00:01:14.430 --> 00:01:16.109
And that means you have to define

00:01:16.110 --> 00:01:17.129
your problem.

00:01:17.130 --> 00:01:18.779
It's not just a matter of figuring

00:01:18.780 --> 00:01:19.829
out the symptoms.

00:01:19.830 --> 00:01:21.839
The symptom is my "computing

00:01:21.840 --> 00:01:23.459
environment stopped being available"

00:01:23.460 --> 00:01:25.379
or "the database dropped

00:01:25.380 --> 00:01:26.939
offline". That's a symptom.

00:01:26.940 --> 00:01:28.559
That's not identifying what the

00:01:28.560 --> 00:01:29.969
problem is.

00:01:29.970 --> 00:01:32.039
The first step in that RCA

00:01:32.040 --> 00:01:33.929
is figure out what

00:01:33.930 --> 00:01:35.189
it is that went wrong.

00:01:35.190 --> 00:01:37.589
And so, in order to identify

00:01:37.590 --> 00:01:39.659
what went wrong, your second

00:01:39.660 --> 00:01:41.129
step, which is really related to the

00:01:41.130 --> 00:01:44.189
first, is you must collect

00:01:44.190 --> 00:01:45.190
data.

00:01:46.350 --> 00:01:47.849
Because the decisions that you're

00:01:47.850 --> 00:01:49.649
going to make as part of this RCA

00:01:49.650 --> 00:01:51.479
process have to

00:01:51.480 --> 00:01:52.529
be based in data.

00:01:52.530 --> 00:01:54.329
They can't be based and guess they

00:01:54.330 --> 00:01:57.239
can't be based in

00:01:57.240 --> 00:01:58.139
conjecture.

00:01:58.140 --> 00:02:00.269
You have to know what

00:02:00.270 --> 00:02:01.529
went wrong and you have to have the

00:02:01.530 --> 00:02:03.329
data to back that up.

00:02:03.330 --> 00:02:05.069
Sometimes you get a little glitch

00:02:05.070 --> 00:02:07.289
that resolves itself automatically.

00:02:07.290 --> 00:02:08.339
You're still going to want to run an

00:02:08.340 --> 00:02:10.319
RCA process on that and

00:02:10.320 --> 00:02:12.119
you want to know what caused the

00:02:12.120 --> 00:02:13.619
glitch. You can't just trust that

00:02:13.620 --> 00:02:15.419
it's going to magically fix itself

00:02:15.420 --> 00:02:16.229
in the future.

00:02:16.230 --> 00:02:18.719
So you must collect data.

00:02:18.720 --> 00:02:20.879
Now, next step is

00:02:20.880 --> 00:02:22.199
you have identified your problem,

00:02:22.200 --> 00:02:23.159
right? You've defined it.

00:02:23.160 --> 00:02:24.539
You've got your data.

00:02:24.540 --> 00:02:27.059
You now have to ask why,

00:02:27.060 --> 00:02:28.979
and asking why is more than

00:02:28.980 --> 00:02:30.149
just asking the question.

00:02:30.150 --> 00:02:31.979
You have to make causal

00:02:31.980 --> 00:02:33.269
connections.

00:02:33.270 --> 00:02:35.279
So as we're asking

00:02:35.280 --> 00:02:36.280
why,

00:02:38.940 --> 00:02:41.279
we have to get really in-depth,

00:02:41.280 --> 00:02:43.259
right? It can't just be

00:02:43.260 --> 00:02:45.089
"all right, well, the power

00:02:45.090 --> 00:02:47.009
went out and as a result, these

00:02:47.010 --> 00:02:48.869
breakers tripped, and then when

00:02:48.870 --> 00:02:50.309
the power came back on, the breakers

00:02:50.310 --> 00:02:51.749
didn't automatically reset".

00:02:51.750 --> 00:02:53.309
You have to know, why didn't they

00:02:53.310 --> 00:02:54.749
automatically reset?

00:02:54.750 --> 00:02:56.699
Is it because you weren't doing

00:02:56.700 --> 00:02:57.719
your preventative maintenance

00:02:57.720 --> 00:02:59.159
correctly or you were doing your

00:02:59.160 --> 00:03:00.929
preventative maintenance correctly,

00:03:00.930 --> 00:03:02.849
but the schedule wasn't right,

00:03:02.850 --> 00:03:04.439
or even though you were doing the

00:03:04.440 --> 00:03:05.759
preventative maintenance and the

00:03:05.760 --> 00:03:07.739
schedule was right, maybe

00:03:07.740 --> 00:03:08.969
the equipment just failed.

00:03:08.970 --> 00:03:10.529
Did it fail inservice?

00:03:10.530 --> 00:03:12.389
Did it fail out of service?

00:03:12.390 --> 00:03:13.439
Do you need to go back to the

00:03:13.440 --> 00:03:15.149
manufacturer of that equipment and

00:03:15.150 --> 00:03:17.189
find out what on earth is going on?

00:03:17.190 --> 00:03:19.079
Because things did not happen

00:03:19.080 --> 00:03:20.879
the way that they should have.

00:03:20.880 --> 00:03:22.619
You have to ask why and you have to

00:03:22.620 --> 00:03:24.599
make those causal connections.

00:03:24.600 --> 00:03:25.889
And remember, one of the biggest

00:03:25.890 --> 00:03:27.929
goals of the RCA is to

00:03:27.930 --> 00:03:29.909
not only figure out what you

00:03:29.910 --> 00:03:32.369
did in order to solve the problem,

00:03:32.370 --> 00:03:34.439
you have to figure out how to

00:03:34.440 --> 00:03:36.089
keep it from happening again.

00:03:36.090 --> 00:03:37.679
If you don't get to that point,

00:03:37.680 --> 00:03:39.839
there's no point in doing an RCA,

00:03:39.840 --> 00:03:41.489
it's just a paperwork exercise.

00:03:41.490 --> 00:03:43.259
Why even bother with it?

00:03:43.260 --> 00:03:45.149
So we've identified what went

00:03:45.150 --> 00:03:46.739
wrong. We've got our data.

00:03:46.740 --> 00:03:48.539
We've asked why, we've made those

00:03:48.540 --> 00:03:50.069
causal connections between

00:03:50.070 --> 00:03:51.719
everything that went wrong to figure

00:03:51.720 --> 00:03:53.159
out what happened.

00:03:53.160 --> 00:03:55.049
Because remember, in the world

00:03:55.050 --> 00:03:57.059
of technology, our problems

00:03:57.060 --> 00:03:58.979
are very, very rarely a

00:03:58.980 --> 00:03:59.879
single thing.

00:03:59.880 --> 00:04:01.919
It is almost always a cascading

00:04:01.920 --> 00:04:03.839
error of some kind that

00:04:03.840 --> 00:04:05.699
started with something simple

00:04:05.700 --> 00:04:08.009
and cascaded into something serious.

00:04:08.010 --> 00:04:09.719
So we've made those causal

00:04:09.720 --> 00:04:11.009
connections.

00:04:11.010 --> 00:04:12.989
Now we have to actually figure out

00:04:12.990 --> 00:04:14.459
what are we going to fix.

00:04:14.460 --> 00:04:17.040
Right. We are going to identify

00:04:19.440 --> 00:04:20.610
those corrections.

00:04:24.550 --> 00:04:26.619
So we've identified

00:04:26.620 --> 00:04:28.419
what it is that we're going to fix,

00:04:28.420 --> 00:04:30.279
and solving it

00:04:30.280 --> 00:04:32.079
isn't just a matter of figuring out

00:04:32.080 --> 00:04:33.489
what it is you're going to fix.

00:04:33.490 --> 00:04:35.349
You've also got to figure out

00:04:35.350 --> 00:04:36.939
how to keep it from happening again.

00:04:36.940 --> 00:04:38.499
Right. And so once you've identified

00:04:38.500 --> 00:04:40.629
your corrections, a huge, huge

00:04:40.630 --> 00:04:42.339
piece of that is that you need to

00:04:42.340 --> 00:04:44.499
figure out what defects did you find

00:04:44.500 --> 00:04:46.449
in your data collection stage.

00:04:46.450 --> 00:04:48.399
So do you need to improve your

00:04:48.400 --> 00:04:49.400
monitoring?

00:04:50.300 --> 00:04:52.149
Are you collecting all of the

00:04:52.150 --> 00:04:53.089
things that you need?

00:04:53.090 --> 00:04:55.899
Are you also logging it?

00:04:55.900 --> 00:04:57.159
Did you figure out that you're

00:04:57.160 --> 00:04:58.509
actually monitoring the things

00:04:58.510 --> 00:04:59.979
correctly, but you're not storing

00:04:59.980 --> 00:05:01.839
that data because monitoring

00:05:01.840 --> 00:05:03.699
and logging go hand in hand?

00:05:03.700 --> 00:05:05.769
There is no point in having active

00:05:05.770 --> 00:05:07.869
monitoring that you don't also save

00:05:07.870 --> 00:05:09.039
so that you can do this kind of

00:05:09.040 --> 00:05:10.209
analysis later.

00:05:10.210 --> 00:05:11.439
Likewise, there's no point in

00:05:11.440 --> 00:05:13.149
logging data that no one is ever

00:05:13.150 --> 00:05:14.019
looking at.

00:05:14.020 --> 00:05:15.459
So they are hand in hand.

00:05:15.460 --> 00:05:16.809
And that's a big piece of

00:05:16.810 --> 00:05:18.249
identifying the corrections,

00:05:18.250 --> 00:05:20.079
identifying what you are going to

00:05:20.080 --> 00:05:21.279
fix.

00:05:21.280 --> 00:05:23.319
All right. So we've found our gaps

00:05:23.320 --> 00:05:25.209
in monitoring. We found our gaps in

00:05:25.210 --> 00:05:26.079
logging.

00:05:26.080 --> 00:05:27.099
We figured out what other

00:05:27.100 --> 00:05:28.779
corrections it is that we need to

00:05:28.780 --> 00:05:29.889
make now.

00:05:29.890 --> 00:05:30.759
What do we do now?

00:05:30.760 --> 00:05:32.079
We've got to implement the solution,

00:05:32.080 --> 00:05:33.849
right. Now is the implementation

00:05:33.850 --> 00:05:35.109
phase.

00:05:35.110 --> 00:05:36.110
So,

00:05:40.620 --> 00:05:42.479
implementation. We have

00:05:42.480 --> 00:05:44.249
to implement not just the short term

00:05:44.250 --> 00:05:45.959
fix that we use to solve the outage

00:05:45.960 --> 00:05:47.819
that we had, but we also have to

00:05:47.820 --> 00:05:49.289
implement all of the long term

00:05:49.290 --> 00:05:51.149
things, right, monitoring, logging,

00:05:51.150 --> 00:05:52.979
other corrections, software defects.

00:05:52.980 --> 00:05:54.329
Maybe we have a change management

00:05:54.330 --> 00:05:55.379
problem.

00:05:55.380 --> 00:05:57.329
You have to implement all

00:05:57.330 --> 00:05:59.429
of those fixes and you have

00:05:59.430 --> 00:06:01.169
to get them out there, because

00:06:01.170 --> 00:06:03.329
there's no point in doing the work

00:06:03.330 --> 00:06:04.889
to just let it sit, right?

00:06:04.890 --> 00:06:06.479
If you write up the RCA and you

00:06:06.480 --> 00:06:08.189
don't implement all of the changes

00:06:08.190 --> 00:06:10.349
that you need in order to

00:06:10.350 --> 00:06:12.059
make sure it doesn't happen again,

00:06:12.060 --> 00:06:13.169
again, it's a paperwork exercise.

00:06:13.170 --> 00:06:15.629
Not worth identifying the time.

00:06:15.630 --> 00:06:17.399
And then the last step, and this is

00:06:17.400 --> 00:06:19.409
the one that is often the hardest

00:06:19.410 --> 00:06:21.299
for everyone involved in any

00:06:21.300 --> 00:06:23.279
kind of a customer impacting

00:06:23.280 --> 00:06:25.919
event, is communication.

00:06:25.920 --> 00:06:27.479
So I'm just going to put this up

00:06:27.480 --> 00:06:28.480
here as "comms".

00:06:29.280 --> 00:06:30.209
And I'm going to underline it,

00:06:30.210 --> 00:06:31.229
actually, I'm going to underline it

00:06:31.230 --> 00:06:32.369
twice.

00:06:32.370 --> 00:06:35.219
Communication is so important

00:06:35.220 --> 00:06:36.839
because once you have figured out

00:06:36.840 --> 00:06:38.459
what the problem is, you figured out

00:06:38.460 --> 00:06:39.749
how you're going to fix it, and you

00:06:39.750 --> 00:06:41.129
figured out what gaps and defects

00:06:41.130 --> 00:06:42.419
you have, you've implemented those

00:06:42.420 --> 00:06:44.249
gaps and defects, you have to

00:06:44.250 --> 00:06:46.139
keep your stakeholders apprized

00:06:46.140 --> 00:06:47.459
of what is going on.

00:06:47.460 --> 00:06:49.049
And it's hard for us to admit that

00:06:49.050 --> 00:06:50.429
things went wrong. It's hard for us

00:06:50.430 --> 00:06:51.749
to admit that there were failures

00:06:51.750 --> 00:06:53.099
that were our fault, that we have

00:06:53.100 --> 00:06:55.239
acknowledged and we're going to fix.

00:06:55.240 --> 00:06:57.539
And so if we think about a company

00:06:57.540 --> 00:06:59.789
culture, comms

00:06:59.790 --> 00:07:01.739
around RCAs are so

00:07:01.740 --> 00:07:02.969
important because it is

00:07:02.970 --> 00:07:04.589
acknowledging to your customers,

00:07:04.590 --> 00:07:06.299
"yes, we're not perfect".

00:07:06.300 --> 00:07:08.159
We made a mistake, or

00:07:08.160 --> 00:07:09.749
the vendors that we selected had a

00:07:09.750 --> 00:07:11.609
problem, or really

00:07:11.610 --> 00:07:13.589
just about anything that

00:07:13.590 --> 00:07:15.269
could go wrong will eventually go

00:07:15.270 --> 00:07:17.639
wrong. But we are reassuring

00:07:17.640 --> 00:07:19.589
you through our communication in

00:07:19.590 --> 00:07:21.479
this RCA process that

00:07:21.480 --> 00:07:23.249
we know that things happen and we

00:07:23.250 --> 00:07:24.719
are going to fix it and ensure that

00:07:24.720 --> 00:07:26.849
it never happens again.

00:07:26.850 --> 00:07:28.589
This comms piece is the most

00:07:28.590 --> 00:07:29.379
important part.

00:07:29.380 --> 00:07:30.779
You can't just write up a two or

00:07:30.780 --> 00:07:32.819
three sentence, "Yes, something

00:07:32.820 --> 00:07:34.799
broke. We have identified fixes.

00:07:34.800 --> 00:07:35.969
We've implemented them and we'll

00:07:35.970 --> 00:07:37.709
make sure it doesn't happen again".

00:07:37.710 --> 00:07:39.599
You need to go one level further

00:07:39.600 --> 00:07:41.549
than that. You have to restore

00:07:41.550 --> 00:07:43.139
that trust and restore your

00:07:43.140 --> 00:07:45.449
customers confidence in you

00:07:45.450 --> 00:07:47.309
that you are acknowledging

00:07:47.310 --> 00:07:48.749
that you are not perfect and you're

00:07:48.750 --> 00:07:50.399
going to fix things so that they

00:07:50.400 --> 00:07:52.019
don't happen again.

00:07:52.020 --> 00:07:54.179
And then you've got to keep

00:07:54.180 --> 00:07:55.769
that communication going.

00:07:55.770 --> 00:07:56.759
Once you've reached this

00:07:56.760 --> 00:07:58.169
implementation phase and you've

00:07:58.170 --> 00:08:00.149
actually got the problem rolled,

00:08:00.150 --> 00:08:02.309
out at that point

00:08:02.310 --> 00:08:03.569
keep up with your customers.

00:08:03.570 --> 00:08:05.279
If somebody had a CIE six months

00:08:05.280 --> 00:08:06.629
ago, reach out to them.

00:08:06.630 --> 00:08:07.979
Be sure that they're still OK.

00:08:07.980 --> 00:08:09.959
Be sure that they have accepted

00:08:09.960 --> 00:08:11.729
the plan that you gave them on how

00:08:11.730 --> 00:08:13.709
it's never going to happen again and

00:08:13.710 --> 00:08:15.449
be sure that they are OK with it and

00:08:15.450 --> 00:08:17.249
that it's solving their needs.

00:08:17.250 --> 00:08:18.329
So thank you so much.

00:08:18.330 --> 00:08:19.829
Hopefully this was helpful to you.

00:08:19.830 --> 00:08:20.879
If you have any questions or

00:08:20.880 --> 00:08:22.439
comments, please feel free to share

00:08:22.440 --> 00:08:23.759
them with us below.

00:08:23.760 --> 00:08:25.289
If you enjoyed this video and you

00:08:25.290 --> 00:08:26.519
would like to see more like it in

00:08:26.520 --> 00:08:28.379
the future, please do like the video

00:08:28.380 --> 00:08:29.909
and subscribe to us so that we'll

00:08:29.910 --> 00:08:31.470
know to keep creating for you.

